---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('mailchimp')!;
const b = appBySlug('recart')!;
const path = '/compare/mailchimp-vs-recart';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/mailchimp-vs-recart');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/mailchimp-vs-recart');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Is Mailchimp email-only?",
    "answer": "No. Mailchimp documents abandoned-cart email and SMS workflows, with supported-store and SMS program requirements. Check eligibility in the actual account."
  },
  {
    "question": "What makes Recart a different operating choice?",
    "answer": "Recart presents Shopify SMS flows and a dedicated SMS strategist. Evaluate the service scope and responsibility for approvals, suppression and reporting."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can both tools claim the same recovered order?",
    "answer": "Their reports may overlap. Reconcile by Shopify order and evaluate incremental profit rather than adding attributed totals."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should the old SMS sender stay active during migration?",
    "answer": "Retain records and a rollback plan, but assign one active owner for each SMS flow so parallel systems do not send duplicate reminders."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
<a href="/compare" class="link">← Comparisons</a>
<h1 class="hero-title mt-6">{a.name} vs {b.name} for Shopify cart recovery</h1>
<p class="mt-7 text-xl">Decide who operates SMS alongside Mailchimp.</p>
<TldrBox picks={[{"label": "Evaluate", "tool": "Mailchimp", "reason": "Own the store-connected messaging program."}, {"label": "Evaluate", "tool": "Recart", "reason": "Assign managed SMS responsibilities."}]} />
<article class="prose-shopify mt-12 max-w-4xl">

<h2>Compare the operating model before adding another sender</h2>
<p>Start with the people who will run the program. Mailchimp may already own your email audience and store-connected recovery; it also documents SMS. Recart is worth evaluating when you want a Shopify SMS specialist and help operating the channel. Adding it should solve an ownership or execution gap, with explicit boundaries around the email program you retain.</p>
<p><a href="https://recart.com/">Recart’s current product page</a> lists SMS list growth, abandoned-cart and browse flows, and a dedicated SMS strategist. These are supplier-described capabilities, not evidence that SMS outperforms email for your store. Ask for a written service scope covering setup, campaign planning, creative approval, monitoring and escalation when an automation misfires.</p>
<h2>Verify the store connection and recovery eligibility</h2>
<p><a href="https://mailchimp.com/help/create-abandoned-cart-customer-journey/">Mailchimp documents abandoned-cart email and SMS flows</a> for supported connected stores. SMS requires an approved program; check the integration’s supported features and your plan before assigning either channel. The guide distinguishes initial recovery from additional subscribed-contact sends. Do not treat an email signup, a phone number or a past purchase as interchangeable permission for every follow-up.</p>
<p><a href="https://mailchimp.com/help/connect-shopify/">Mailchimp’s Shopify connection guide</a> calls for recreating existing abandoned-cart automations and reviewing welcome automations when connecting. Inventory current Shopify and app sends before enabling a replacement. Use a fresh checkout with an identifiable test customer to verify the trigger, recovered cart link, purchase exit and actual destination audience. These are proposed acceptance checks, not results from a vendor trial.</p>
<h2>Make the strategist’s remit concrete</h2>
<p>Agree who proposes the offer, who approves it, who can pause a flow and who responds to customer replies. Ask which work the strategist performs and which work stays with your team or agency. Have a named backup for holidays and launches. “Managed” is only useful if someone can correct a broken cart link or stop an inappropriate promotion when the store changes.</p>
<p>Take a subscription cart, a financing or BNPL checkout and an international order to the onboarding discussion. Ask the operator to show how each would be handled in your actual configuration. A reminder should explain recurring terms, available payment support or delivered cost as appropriate. Do not authorize urgency claims or generic discounts that conceal the shopper’s underlying obstacle.</p>
<h2>Choose one SMS owner and coordinate email deliberately</h2>
<p>Make a message inventory by trigger, channel, audience and sender: welcome, browse, cart, post-purchase and promotional campaigns. If Recart owns cart SMS, disable the equivalent SMS flow elsewhere before it goes live. Keep email ownership explicit. A simultaneous email and text offering different discounts creates both support work and unreliable measurement.</p>
<p>Record permission provenance separately for email and SMS and reconcile opt-outs before importing an existing list. Test a subscribed number, an opted-out number, a fresh opt-in and an email-only customer. Agree on quiet hours, frequency limits and cross-channel exclusions for the markets you actually serve. Confirm these settings in the account; the existence of a strategist is not proof that your configuration is correct.</p>
<h2>Run a purchase-exit rehearsal before campaigns</h2>
<p>Use a controlled customer journey: abandon an eligible checkout, inspect the pending messages, then purchase before the next step. Verify that no stale recovery message arrives after the order. Repeat after modifying a variant or quantity and confirm the link reflects a useful cart. An unavailable size should receive an appropriate alternative or no promotion, not a promise that the original basket can still be bought.</p>
<p>Also inspect a repeat customer, a first-time mobile visitor and a gift purchase. Check that first-order codes do not reach ineligible buyers, that the mobile checkout opens correctly and that sizing or gift support is accessible. For refunds and returns, decide whether pending recovery and promotional messages need exclusion; an order-status event is not automatically a reason to restart acquisition messaging.</p>
<h2>Set an offer budget and evaluate the managed fee</h2>
<p>Hypothetical fixed-goods-cost examples: a $361 basket at 67% gross margin starts with $241.87 gross profit. A 15% code costs $54.15, leaving $187.72. A $483 basket at 55% margin starts with $265.65; an 8% code costs $38.64, leaving $227.01. Shipping, returns, payment fees, message charges and service costs are excluded. These are planning examples, not observed vendor results.</p>
<p>For a bundle, test whether a recovery code stacks with the existing promotion. For an MOQ problem, offer threshold guidance rather than discounting an invalid order. Request a quote using your reachable SMS audience, sending countries, projected message volume and service scope. Compare the total cost with the staff time saved and incremental gross profit; do not choose from stale entry prices or unsupported ratings.</p>
<h2>Report one order once and retain a rollback owner</h2>
<p>Reconcile Mailchimp and Recart attributed orders against Shopify order identifiers. Report audience size, sends, unsubscribes, discount expense and completed orders alongside revenue. A controlled no-recovery group can help estimate incrementality if volume supports it; decide the design before launch rather than asserting a universal attribution correction percentage.</p>
<p>For cutover, preserve permission records, approved templates and current settings. Pause the replaced SMS flows, enable the agreed replacement and inspect fresh events before expanding sends. If purchase exits or exclusions fail, pause the new flow and use the documented rollback plan. Never reactivate both SMS owners merely to avoid a short gap. Sources checked November 25, 2026.</p>
</article>
<FaqSection faqs={faqs} title={`${a.name} vs ${b.name} FAQ`} />
</div></section>
</BaseLayout>
